{
  "description": "Named SCOPE PREDICATES — the reusable WHERE-clause half of the semantic layer, kept separate from measures. A KPI config answers 'what do we aggregate'; a predicate answers 'over which subset'. A user phrase like 'major' or 'sla breached' is a predicate, not a metric, so it must never be resolved to a KPI (see MULTI_METRIC_ANALYSIS.md section 1).\n\nEach predicate declares per-table BINDINGS because the same business concept is stored differently per table: sla_breached is integer 1 on itsm_incident.tbl_incident_sla, varchar '1' on tbl_sla_response, boolean true on tbl_tableau_major_incdnt and varchar 'TRUE' on the service-request table. A binding with an empty `conditions` list means the table is ALREADY scoped that way (e.g. every row of tbl_major_incidents is a major incident), so the predicate costs no WHERE clause at all — it is satisfied by choosing that table.\n\nVALUES HERE ARE DB-VERIFIED and are used VERBATIM: the composer marks them `resolved` so sql_builder skips value_resolver. This is deliberate — schema_v3.yaml `possible_values` were found to disagree with the database on many columns (e.g. active_indicator_type is NOT ['NO','YES']), so a predicate must not be re-resolved against that domain. Re-verify with `python -m cora_mcp.predicate_registry --verify` after any data model change.\n\n`grain_key` is the column that identifies one entity instance across tables; it is the join key used when a predicate cannot bind to the anchor table and has to become an EXISTS semi-join.",
  "predicates": {
    "major_incident": {
      "synonyms": ["major", "major incident", "major incidents", "mi", "major inc",
                   "sev1", "sev 1", "severity 1", "critical incident", "critical incidents"],
      "entity": "incident",
      "grain_key": "incident_id",
      "bindings": [
        {"table": "itsm_incident.tbl_major_incidents", "conditions": [], "primary": true,
         "note": "PRIMARY: dedicated major-incident table, so the predicate costs no WHERE clause at all (verified: all 1651 rows have major_incident_indicator='1')"},
        {"table": "itsm_availability.tbl_tableau_major_incdnt", "conditions": [],
         "note": "major-incident SLA table; 1:1 with tbl_major_incidents on incident_id"},
        {"table": "itsm_incident.tbl_all_incidents",
         "conditions": [{"field": "major_incident_indicator", "op": "=", "values": [true]}],
         "note": "boolean column; verified true=1180 / false=154280"}
      ]
    },
    "sla_breached": {
      "synonyms": ["sla breached", "sla breach", "sla breaches", "breached sla", "breached",
                   "missed sla", "sla missed", "out of sla", "outside sla", "sla violation",
                   "sla violated", "not within sla"],
      "entity": "incident",
      "grain_key": "incident_id",
      "negated_by": "sla_met",
      "bindings": [
        {"table": "itsm_incident.tbl_incident_sla", "primary": true,
         "conditions": [{"field": "sla_breached_indicator", "op": "=", "values": [1]}],
         "note": "PRIMARY: the only incident SLA table covering BOTH the response and resolution clocks (310920 rows), so a generic 'breached' question must resolve here rather than to one clock. Integer column; verified 1=67088 / 0=243832."},
        {"table": "itsm_incident.tbl_sla_resolution",
         "conditions": [{"field": "sla_breached_indicator", "op": "=", "values": [1]}],
         "note": "integer column; verified 1=331 / 0=135518 (resolution clock only)"},
        {"table": "itsm_incident.tbl_sla_response",
         "conditions": [{"field": "sla_breached_indicator", "op": "=", "values": ["1"]}],
         "note": "varchar column; verified '1'=18624 / '0'=136836 (response clock only)"},
        {"table": "itsm_availability.tbl_tableau_major_incdnt",
         "conditions": [{"field": "sla_breached_indicator", "op": "=", "values": [true]}],
         "note": "boolean column; verified true=617 / false=1034"}
      ]
    },
    "sr_sla_breached": {
      "synonyms": ["request sla breached", "sr sla breached", "request breached sla",
                   "service request sla breach", "request missed sla"],
      "entity": "service_request",
      "grain_key": "request_item_id",
      "note": "Deliberately SEPARATE from sla_breached: the service-request grain key is request_item_id, not incident_id, so the two must never be intersected or semi-joined into one another.",
      "bindings": [
        {"table": "itsm_servicerequest.tbl_request_item", "primary": true,
         "conditions": [{"field": "sla_breached_indicator", "op": "=", "values": ["TRUE"]}],
         "note": "varchar UPPERCASE column; verified 'TRUE'=9350 / 'FALSE'=42850"}
      ]
    },
    "sla_met": {
      "synonyms": ["sla met", "met sla", "within sla", "inside sla", "sla compliant",
                   "sla compliance", "not breached", "no breach", "sla honoured", "sla honored"],
      "entity": "incident",
      "grain_key": "incident_id",
      "negated_by": "sla_breached",
      "bindings": [
        {"table": "itsm_incident.tbl_incident_sla", "primary": true,
         "conditions": [{"field": "sla_breached_indicator", "op": "=", "values": [0]}],
